  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Colletes americanus, commonly known as the American Cellophane Bee, is a solitary bee species native to New England that belongs to the Colletidae family, known as plasterer or polyester bees. These bees are important native pollinators that line their nesting tunnels with a cellophane-like secretion, giving the family its distinctive name.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cellophane-like nest lining",
      "description": "The bee secretes a clear, plastic-like substance to line its underground nesting tunnels, which is diagnostic of the Colletidae family",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and coloration",
      "description": "Small bee, approximately 8-10mm in length, with dark brown to black body and sparse hair",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bilobed tongue",
      "description": "Possesses a distinctively forked or bilobed tongue, shorter and less prominent than long-tongued bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "American Cellophane Bees are found in sandy or loose soil areas throughout New England, where they can excavate nesting burrows in open ground, gardens, and disturbed areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike honeybees, Colletes americanus are solitary bees that each female digs and provisions her own individual nest tunnel, and she lines it with a remarkable cellophane-like waterproof secretion that protects her larvae from moisture and parasites.",
